---
import {Image} from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../styles/accessibility.scss";
import bg from "../../assets/images/accessibility-bg.svg"
import doajLogin from "../../assets/images/project-notes/doajAccessibilitylogin.png";
import logo from "../../assets/images/doaj/logo-full.png";
---

<BaseLayout title="Accessible login and registration">
    <main class="container case-study case-study--accessible-login" id="main-content">
        <article class="case-study project-note">
            <div class="hero hero--project hero--project--accessible-login">
                <p class="hero--project__client">
                    <span class="sr-only">Directory of Open Access Journals</span>
                    <Image src={logo} format="webp"
                           alt=""/>
                </p>

                <h1 class="hero--project__description easy-read">
                    Removing barriers from login and registration
                </h1>

                <p class="hero--project__summary easy-read">
                    A focused review of the accessibility requirements introduced in WCAG 2.2 led me to
                    improve two parts of DOAJ’s account journey: introducing passwordless login to reduce
                    reliance on memory and replacing a CAPTCHA that could present users with an
                    inaccessible challenge.
                </p>

                <p class="tag">
                    <span>Accessibility and implementation</span>
                </p>
            </div>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <div class="easy-read">
                    <h2>Looking beyond visible accessibility</h2>

                    <p>
                        I was asked to review a new release of DOAJ against the criteria introduced in WCAG
                        2.2. The review drew my attention to the Accessible Authentication guidance, which
                        addresses the barriers created when people must remember, transcribe or solve something
                        to access a service.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        DOAJ’s login process relied on users entering a password. Passwords are not inherently
                        non-compliant, but they can create avoidable difficulty for people with cognitive
                        disabilities. I recommended providing a passwordless email-link option so users could
                        access their account without having to recall or transcribe a password.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Registration presented a different problem. DOAJ used reCAPTCHA v2, which usually
                        displayed an “I’m not a robot” checkbox but could require an image-recognition challenge
                        when Google considered a user’s behaviour suspicious. This created a particular risk
                        for disabled people whose input methods or interaction patterns differed from those the
                        system expected.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The two issues were related, but required different solutions: passwordless login could
                        make authentication easier, while registration still needed protection against
                        automated spam without asking users to prove that they were human.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <div class="easy-read">
                    <h2>Comparing accessibility, privacy and complexity</h2>

                    <p>
                        Rather than recommending the first invisible alternative, I compared three approaches
                        to registration security:
                    </p>

                    <ul class="custom-list">
                        <li>
                            <strong>Retain reCAPTCHA v2:</strong> already implemented and effective against
                            automated submissions, but capable of presenting users with an inaccessible
                            challenge.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Move to reCAPTCHA v3:</strong> invisible during normal use, but dependent on
                            continuous behavioural scoring. It introduced privacy questions and required
                            additional decisions and backend handling for users given a suspicious score.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Use a honeypot:</strong> add a hidden trap field and submission-timing check
                            that bots are likely to trigger, without giving legitimate users another task to
                            complete. It was simpler and more private, although it required careful calibration
                            and would not prevent deliberate manual spam.
                        </li>
                    </ul>

                    <p>
                        I recommended the honeypot approach after considering accessibility, privacy, user
                        friction and implementation effort together. It addressed the service’s actual need—to
                        detect automated submissions—without evaluating a user’s ability to complete a puzzle.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table-wrapper" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
                    <table class="comparison-table">
                        <caption class="sr-only">
                            Bot protection techniques comparison
                        </caption>

                        <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="col">Consideration</th>
                            <th scope="col">reCAPTCHA v2</th>
                            <th scope="col">reCAPTCHA v3</th>
                            <th scope="col" class="recommended">
                                Honeypot
                                <span class="comparison-table__recommendation">
                                    Recommended
                                </span>
                            </th>
                        </tr>
                        </thead>

                        <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row">User interaction</th>
                            <td>
                                Checkbox, with a possible image or audio challenge
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Usually invisible
                            </td>
                            <td class="recommended">
                                No user-facing task
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row">Accessibility</th>
                            <td>
                                The challenge can create cognitive, visual and motor barriers
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                No puzzle by default, but suspicious scores still need handling
                            </td>
                            <td class="recommended">
                                No visible verification step
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row">Data and privacy</th>
                            <td>
                                Third-party risk assessment
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Continuous behavioural scoring
                            </td>
                            <td class="recommended">
                                First-party form and timing signals
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row">Implementation</th>
                            <td>
                                Already implemented
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Additional backend decisions and fallback workflow
                            </td>
                            <td class="recommended">
                                Small front- and backend change, followed by calibration
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row">Limitation</th>
                            <td>
                                Can obstruct legitimate users
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Privacy and false-positive concerns
                            </td>
                            <td class="recommended">
                                Less effective against sophisticated or manual spam
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr class="comparison-table__decision">
                            <th scope="row">Decision</th>
                            <td>Not selected</td>
                            <td>Not selected</td>
                            <td class="recommended">
                                <strong>Recommended</strong>
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section easy-read">
                    <h2>Designing and implementing the alternatives</h2>

                    <p>
                        I redesigned the login journey to include passwordless access by email. A user can
                        provide their email address, receive a secure link and use it to access their account
                        without entering a password.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        For registration, I implemented the front-end part of the honeypot: fields and
                        interaction behaviour that remain hidden from legitimate users but provide signals that
                        can identify automated submissions. A colleague implemented the corresponding
                        server-side validation.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Both changes used DOAJ’s established design system. This allowed us to
                        improve the underlying behaviour without making the account journey feel like a
                        separate or newly redesigned part of the service.
                    </p>

                    <figure class="case-study-figure case-study-figure--doaj-login">
                        <Image src={doajLogin} format="webp"
                               alt=""/>
                        <figcaption class="board-note">
                            The honeypot protects registration without adding a visible challenge, while passwordless access
                            gives
                            users an alternative to entering a password.
                        </figcaption>
                    </figure>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <div class="easy-read">
                    <h2>Result</h2>

                    <p>
                        Users can now access their account through an emailed link, and registration no longer
                        depends on an interactive CAPTCHA. Automated checks take place in the background rather
                        than requiring users to recognise images or complete another verification task.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The rate of new-account creation remained steady after the honeypot was introduced,
                        indicating that removing the visible challenge did not disrupt legitimate
                        registrations.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        What began as a focused standards review resulted in two implemented improvements. My
                        contribution extended beyond identifying the accessibility risks: I investigated the
                        available approaches, assessed their wider trade-offs, recommended a direction,
                        designed the new interactions and implemented the front end.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>
        </article>
        <img
                src={bg.src}
                alt=""
                class="background"
        />
    </main>
</BaseLayout>
